o eat  healthier i'm really not about  perfection  i am about realistic mealtime solutions  and for a lot of busy folks this is a  really great option  i also think that it helps a lot of  people get out of cooking ruts  so even if you're leaning on these kits  for short stints  just get some more cooking inspiration i  think that there's value in that too  anyway i think that beatrice's choice is  one of the more nutritious options that  i've seen  so she's got some really good quality  protein and fat from the chicken  and the vinaigrette plus tons of fiber  rich  veg from the kale salad and mushrooms i  would say that the meal  is lower in carbs which beatrice  actually points out herself  saying that she loves her carb but is  trying to limit her carb intake to help  her lose weight  and while this may be working for her as  low carb does  actually work for a lot of dieters i  just want to flag that  you don't necessarily need to cut out  carbs to lose weight  as i talked about in my video reviewing  youtubers weight loss advice right here  at its core weight loss is largely about  eating  in a steady caloric deficit a lot of  people do see more immediate shifts on  the scale starting a low carb diet but  this is largely due to  water loss as the body's glycogen stores  start to deplete  not actually from losing a ton of fat  right out the gates  longer term we do have a large  meta-analysis from this past  december that showed that low carb had a  slight weight loss advantage over low  fat but the difference was actually less  than  three pounds and the low fat folks had  better control  on their cholesterol levels i don't  think that we can make broad assumptions  that one approach is universally better  for weight loss than the other  it likely comes down to individual  differences in metabolism  and also just what feels more  sustainable and easy  to people based on their food  preferences  protein and fat are more satiating than  carbohydrates so  often people feel more full on a lower  carb diet but at the end of the day  it really comes down to maintaining a  caloric deficit  whether that comes at the expense of  dietary carbs  protein or fat now from what i could see  from her other videos and also her other  meals  beatrice doesn't seem to be anti-carb by  any means like  she regularly enjoys foods like cereal  rice  bread without any guilt or like beating  herself  up for it and in the context of this day  she  really might have just been craving a  lower carb lunch  after her oatmeal and berry breakfast so  again if it works for her it works for  me  i just kind of wanted to debunk the carb  hating misinformation out there  cause your girl loves carbs time to  carboload  and on that note let's see what's for  dinner so i did that thing that i  unfortunately do from time to time where  i think i'm recording but in fact  i am not regardless for dinner i had  barbecue pork  green beans and ready rice okay so this  looks like a pretty  balanced plate of protein fat carbs and  veg  it isn't fancy it isn't particularly  super instagramable  but if you're looking for a simple  example of what a balanced plate can  look like  it doesn't get more textbook than this  and i appreciate also that  she's switching it up and including a  non-stereotypical diet protein like  pulled pork  in the mix here that's actually going to  offer a little bit more fat  for some more staying power and probably  also more satisfaction factor as well  because let's be real a girl can only  eat  so much extra lean turkey and chicken  breasts before she totally taps out so  yeah  this looks totally yum to me but let's  take a look at her bedtime snack  and i don't know why but a bowl of  cereal at night time just hits different  it's sweet it's carby it's delicious why  do i crave  the things that are bad for me well not  bad for you  okay so special k with coconut milk and  berries seems like a fine bedtime snack  if you're craving something crunchy  and sweet i am low-key obsessed with  cereal  at all hours of the day so i totally  feel her  on that but there are a few things that  i want to unpack  about beatrice's suggestion that eating  cereal helps her control her sugar  cravings  first as your resident youtube dietitian  i just want to let everyone know  that sugar is not the devil that we are  all taught  that it is is it the most nutrient rich  mineral packed food that you can eat on  the planet  absolutely not but is a little bit of  sugar eaten in moderation bad for you  also absolutely not now while sugar  isn't something  we really need to consume in excess a  sweet snack or two  is not going to make or break your  health bank  especially if you're consuming a  generally varied and balanced diet  in fact it's often this sugar is always  bad for you mentality  that contributes to overwhelming sugar  cravings and overeating in the first  place  classic diet mentality we will always  fantasize about the things we say we  shouldn't  have so i know that beatrice is in a  weight loss phase so  by nature she's going to potentially be  a little restricted  and depending on her level of caloric  restriction  there may be some of that scarcity  mentality going on  but for most people watching especially  those who are not  in an active weight loss phase i really  urge you to try to neutralize our  morality around food when we stop  thinking about food in dichotomous terms  like good versus bad food  we're much less likely to obsess over  and crave the demonized foods  and much more likely to find pleasure in  naturally nourishing foods  so if beatrice is having a sugar craving  before bed it might be a sign that her  body actually needs more carbs  so this could mean allowing herself a  little more carbs  at her snacks and lunch even if it means  a more gradual and steady weight loss  pace  second let's talk about health halos  [Music]  so a health halo is the perception that  a particular food is  good for you even when there is little  or no evidence to confirm that this is  true  special k has a health halo as it's long  been marketed  as a diet friendly option probably  because it's relatively low in calories  and fat free but if we compare the  labels of  special k red berries to let's say a  stereotypical  sugar packed cereal like cinnamon toast  crunch  you can see that they're really not that  different  there's nothing wrong with either of  these cereals so choose whichever one  that you actually  like and it is very possible that  beatrice just loves  special k i like it too but if you're  choosing the diet cereal thinking that  it's  a healthier choice which then just  stimulates the scarcity mentality that  results in a binge it's probably better  to just  have a bowl of cinnamon toast crunch and  call it a day  and on that note let's break down the  stats and  trigger warning i am about to talk  numbers so  feel free to skip ahead to the time  stamp on the screen  if that may be triggering to you so  according to  beatrice's own calorie counting her  nutrition for the day came out to be  about  1543 calories with 31  coming from protein and fat and 38  coming from  carbs she is a little bit low in vitamin  e  d calcium and omega-3 fats but otherwise  she's meeting most of her other  micronutrient needs  i did notice that she takes some of  these as supplements in  another video so i'm happy to hear that  she's got that going on as well  but let's switch gears and discuss what  i really like here  one part of beatrice's videos that  really makes me happy  is her disclaimers where she's talking  about something that maybe is out of her  field of expertise  for example in one of her videos  beatrice is diving into her supplement  routine where she's discussing how  taking fish oil and vitamin d has really  helped to  improve her mood and mental health while  i'm of course glad that those  supplements are  working for her what i love most here is  that she puts a disclaimer at the end of  that segment stating that  she can't definitively attribute being  or feeling better to those supplements  and that she's not a medical  professional period  but she has found that taking these  supplements are helpful  for her because there's so much  misinformation  on the internet in combination with also  a range of people claiming to be experts  in fields that they are not certified in  i always love when someone acknowledges  that the advice that they're giving is  out of their scope  so hats off to you beatrice love that  but as an aside  in case you're wondering she did appear  to get some good advice on the  supplements  as both vitamin d and fish oil have been  shown to play a role in improving  and regulating mood so love that now in  terms of beatrice's approach to weight  loss  i really appreciate that there isn't any  misinformation  about food or demonizing of certain food  groups  in fact beatrice eats all sorts of carbs  and fats and protein  from a variety of sources she also is  not afraid to eat typically  demonized carbs like white bread  especially for somebody who  is trying to lose weight she's really  just trying to eat a variety of  satiating and satisfying foods  that she actually likes while  maintaining a caloric deficit that feels  doable for her  and that to me is probably the best case  scenario  for any kind of weight loss diet it's  not easy to do as it does take  a lot more thinking and planning and  ultimately  some nutrition knowledge but i think in  the end she will be  better equipped to maintain a routine  like this  long term beatrice also discusses her  goals for the future in a different  video  titled six month body transformation  update now in this video she talks about  how she lost  almost 40 pounds so far she goes on to  say that she has a variety of goals to  work towards while she continues her  weight loss efforts  such as beginning strength training  increasing her protein intake and  decreasing her carbs  drinking more water sticking to a 1555  calorie  diet per day and continuing to  prioritize  her mental health so let's break down  some of these goals  first of all i totally support her  inclination to begin  weight training for those of you who may  not know the more  muscle you have the more fat you  potentially can burn  because muscle is metabolically active  and can increase our body's resting  metabolic rate  one study found that 16 weeks of  resistance training  increased participants rmr or resting  metabolic rate  by 6.8 percent and another meta-analysis  found  significantly greater improvements in  rmr for subjects performing resistance  training  compared to aerobic exercise so  definitely a great call on the decision  to lift weights  even if you are not trying to lose  weight second in terms of beatrice's  protein to carb ratio  the research is very clear that in the  macronutrient satiety hierarchy  protein is king because it keeps you  fuller  longer after a meal for a smaller amount  of calories per gram  protein is also thought to help support  a healthy metabolism because  it has a greater thermic effect of food  aka tee  than other macronutrients and therefore  requires  more energy to break down and digest so  for example  protein requires about 20 to 30 percent  of your energy output to digest  while carbs use about five to ten  percent and fats  generally use between zero to three  percent it also is essential for  building  muscle mass and as we already discussed  muscle  is more metabolically active than fat  that being said  carbs are also helpful in the muscle  building equation since  carbs help stimulate an insulin response  to drive the protein into the muscle  faster  so we don't want to demonize carbs but  we also don't want to  overload on protein either too much  protein can also  lead to fat gain and also elicit its own  insulin response so that's ultimately  why  balance is key here next regarding  b